## Migration Analysis

Your code has three patterns that need migration:

| Pattern | Location | Replacement |
|---------|----------|-------------|
| Cancellable work loop | `while (true)` in `RunLoop` | `CancellationToken` check |
| Blocking call interruption | `_newEvent.WaitOne()` | `WaitHandle.WaitAny` with `token.WaitHandle` |
| Thread.Interrupt + Thread.Abort | `Stop()` | `CancellationTokenSource.Cancel()` |

## Migrated Code (.NET 8)

```csharp
public class EventProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private readonly ManualResetEvent _newEvent = new ManualResetEvent(false);
    private readonly Queue<string> _queue = new Queue<string>();
    private Thread? _thread;
    private readonly object _lock = new object();
    private readonly CancellationTokenSource _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();

    public void Start()
    {
        _thread = new Thread(RunLoop);
        _thread.Start();
    }

    private void RunLoop()
    {
        var handles = new WaitHandle[] { _newEvent, _cts.Token.WaitHandle };

        while (!_cts.Token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            // Blocks until either a new event arrives or cancellation is requested
            int signaled = WaitHandle.WaitAny(handles);

            if (signaled == 1) // cancellation token was signaled
                break;

            _newEvent.Reset();

            string? item = null;
            lock (_lock)
            {
                if (_queue.Count > 0)
                    item = _queue.Dequeue();
            }

            if (item is not null)
                Handle(item);
        }
    }

    public void Enqueue(string item)
    {
        lock (_lock) { _queue.Enqueue(item); }
        _newEvent.Set();
    }

    public void Stop()
    {
        _cts.Cancel(); // wakes WaitAny via token.WaitHandle
        _thread?.Join(5000);
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        Stop();
        _cts.Dispose();
        _newEvent.Dispose();
    }
}
```

## Key Changes

1. **`Thread.Abort()` / `Thread.Interrupt()` → `CancellationTokenSource.Cancel()`** — cooperative cancellation replaces forced termination.
2. **`_newEvent.WaitOne()` → `WaitHandle.WaitAny(handles)`** — includes the cancellation token's wait handle so the thread wakes immediately on cancel.
3. **`while (true)` → `while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)`** — loop exits cleanly.
4. **Added `IDisposable`** — `CancellationTokenSource` leaks timers/callbacks if not disposed.

## Pitfalls to Watch

| Pitfall | Why it matters |
|---------|---------------|
| Not using `WaitHandle.WaitAny` | If you only check the token at loop top, the thread stays blocked in `WaitOne()` indefinitely after `Cancel()` — cancellation appears to hang. |
| Race: multiple items queued before `WaitAny` returns | `_newEvent.Set()` coalesces. After waking, drain all available items or you'll lose signals. The code above handles one item per wake; for production, drain the queue in a loop. |
| Not disposing `CancellationTokenSource` | Causes timer and registration leaks. Always wrap in `using` or implement `IDisposable`. |
| Assuming cancellation is instant | `Handle(item)` could run for a long time. If `Handle` does heavy work, pass `_cts.Token` into it and check periodically. |

## Optional: Drain-All Variant

If multiple items can queue between wakes:

```csharp
// After WaitAny returns for _newEvent:
_newEvent.Reset();
lock (_lock)
{
    while (_queue.Count > 0)
    {
        var item = _queue.Dequeue();
        Handle(item);
        if (_cts.Token.IsCancellationRequested) break;
    }
}
```

This prevents lost events from signal coalescing — the most common production bug with this pattern.
